<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Date Hidemune</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1591">1591</a>–<a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Date Hidemune was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> and the first lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a> Domain on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. Born to a concubine and held for years as a hostage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, he was passed over as heir to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a> Domain. In 1614 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> granted him Uwajima, worth 100,000 koku, which his descendants held until 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/date-hidemune.jpg" alt="Date Hidemune" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reggaeman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uwajima_Date_Togakuji_Cemetery_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
